var tafsirData_29 = {
  "1": "\"Alif-Lam-Mim\" begins the chapter of the spider with three recited letters, as \"Ta-Sin-Mim\" began the story of Moses. Again the meaning of the letters is with Allah in its fullest sense, while their work is clear: they mark the speech as Quran, not as a tribal essay on trials.\n\nThis surah will speak of tests, of parents who urge shirk, of Noah, Abraham, Lot, Shu'ayb, of false protectors like a spider's house, and of the recitation that only the knowers truly grasp. The letters are a gate to that whole.\n\nA listener who has just finished Korah's swallowing and the command not to invoke another god is now asked, in the next verse, whether people think a sentence of faith is the end of the road. The letters slow the ear before that challenge. Revelation is about to be severe in mercy, testing those who believe as earlier nations were tested.\n\nHolding the letters without forcing a private code is part of the humility the coming tests will demand. The Book that puts people to trial begins by putting the mind in front of something it cannot decode by desire.",
  "2": "\"Do people think once they say, 'We believe,' that they will be left without being put to the test?\" The question breaks a sweet illusion. \"Iman\" on the tongue is not a certificate that life will go easy. \"La yuftanun,\" that they will not be tried, is the false thought. The word \"fitnah\" here is a test that melts and shows what is inside, like fire on metal.\n\nThe previous chapters showed tests: a mother and a river, a punch and a flight, a queen and a letter, nine men and a plot. This verse says the law is general. Saying \"we believe\" is an entry, not a sofa.\n\nEarly Muslims in Mecca knew this in their bodies: boycott, insult, torture. The verse tells later comfortable believers that they are not a new species. Thought, \"hasiba,\" they reckoned, they assumed. The assumption is corrected.\n\nThe next verse will ground the correction in history: We tested those before them, and Allah will distinguish the truthful from the liars. The test is not cruelty. It is a sorting.",
  "3": "\"We certainly tested those before them.\" The stories just told, and the stories about to be told in this chapter, are evidence. Abraham thrown at a fire, Lot's town, Midian's denial, Mecca's pressure, all sit under \"we tested.\"\n\n\"And in this way Allah will clearly distinguish between those who are truthful and those who are liars.\" \"La-ya'lamanna\" He will surely know, in the sense of making known, distinguishing, showing in the open. The truthful, \"al-sadiqin,\" and the liars, \"al-kadhibin,\" are not sorted by slogans. Tests sort them.\n\nAllah already knows. The distinguishing is for the world and for the people themselves. A person may not know he is a liar about faith until a loss arrives.\n\nThe previous chapter said the Lord knows what hearts conceal. This verse says He will make the concealed visible through fitnah. Knowledge and testing work together.\n\nLiars here are not only people who never believed. They include those who said \"we believe\" as a social password. The next verse turns to evildoers who think they will escape Allah entirely, another bad reckoning.",
  "4": "\"Or do the evildoers simply think that they will escape Us?\" A second \"hasiba,\" a second rotten calculation. If believers thought they would not be tested, criminals think they will outrun Allah. \"Yasbiquna,\" they will precede Us, beat Us, get away.\n\n\"How wrong is their judgment!\" \"Sa'a ma yahkumun,\" how bad is their ruling. Pharaoh thought he would not be returned. Korah thought knowledge made him safe. Both judged badly.\n\nThe verse balances the surah. Tests are not only for the pious. The wicked are not on vacation. Escape is a fantasy.\n\nJudgment, \"yahkumun,\" is what they do with their minds. The chapter will later say Allah judges, and that the spider's house is the frailest. Bad judgment chooses frail houses.\n\nThe next verse addresses the one who hopes for the meeting with Allah: the appointed time is sure, and He is All-Hearing, All-Knowing. Hope, unlike the evildoers' thought of escape, is tied to a coming deadline.",
  "5": "\"Whoever hopes for the meeting with Allah, let them know that Allah's appointed time is sure to come.\" Hope, \"yarju,\" is the believer's stance toward \"liqa' Allah,\" meeting Allah. The \"ajal,\" the appointed term, is coming, \"la-ati.\" Hope is not allowed to become vague poetry. It has a date with a guaranteed arrival, even if the clock is unseen.\n\n\"He is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\" The names \"al-Sami'\" and \"al-Alim\" support the hopeful and warn the faker. Hearing catches the whispered \"we believe.\" Knowledge catches the lie and the truth that tests will show.\n\nMeeting Allah is what Pharaoh's army thought would never happen. This verse makes it the object of hope, not only of fear. People who endure fitnah need a meeting worth the endurance.\n\nThe next verse will speak of striving, \"jahada,\" and say it is for the person's own good, because Allah needs no one. Hope for the meeting becomes work, and the work does not enrich Allah.",
  "6": "\"And whoever strives in Allah's cause, only does so for their own good.\" \"Jahada\" is to exert effort, to struggle, including against the self in a test and against obstacles to faith. The fruit returns to the striver, as gratitude returned to the grateful and guidance to the guided in earlier verses.\n\n\"Surely Allah is not in need of any of His creation.\" \"La-ghaniyyun 'anil-'alamin,\" Self-Sufficient beyond the worlds. The striving does not fill a lack in Allah. Pharaoh's labor of bricks and Korah's keys did not help Allah either. Nothing does. The names Ghani and the worlds together humble every activist.\n\nA misconception is that Allah needs our Islam to win a cosmic vote. This verse kills that idea. Another misconception is that therefore work is pointless. The first half kills that: work is for your own good.\n\nTests, hope for the meeting, and striving form a chain. The next verse promises those who believe and do good that their sins will be absolved and their reward will match the best of what they did.",
  "7": "\"As for those who believe and do good, We will certainly absolve them of their sins, and reward them according to the best of what they used to do.\" After tests and striving, a promise of \"lanukaffiranna,\" We will cover and remove their sayyi'at. Believers who work good are not paid as if every slip were the whole file.\n\n\"According to the best of what they used to do\" is bounty. Payment looks at their finest deeds, \"ahsan alladhi kanoo ya'malun,\" not only at an average dragged down by weakness. The previous chapters said good is rewarded with what is better. This verse adds the wiping of sins and the \"best of\" measure.\n\nThe people who said \"we believe\" and then stood in the test are in view. Liars do not receive this sentence. Truthful strivers do.\n\nAbsolving sins also answers fear that a past like Moses' punch, or any believer's old wrong, makes the meeting hopeless. Belief, good work, and Allah's wiping meet that fear. The next verse will command kindness to parents and then limit obedience if parents push shirk, a test many early Muslims actually met.",
  "8": "\"We have commanded people to honour their parents.\" The test of faith is not an excuse to become harsh at home. \"Wassayna al-insana bi-walidayhi\" is a heavy instruction of ihsan to parents, repeated in several chapters. Honour stands even when the world is on fire with trials.\n\n\"But if they urge you to associate with Me what you have no knowledge of, then do not obey them.\" The limit is shirk. \"Jahadaka\" if they strive, pressure, struggle against you to make you associate what you have no knowledge of—no proof, no revealed basis—then obedience stops. Reports mention Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas and his mother, and other companions whose parents pulled them toward the old gods. The wording is general.\n\n\"To Me you will all return, and then I will inform you of what you used to do.\" Parents and children both go back. Allah will \"unabbi'ukum,\" tell you your deeds. Family pressure does not rewrite the file.\n\nThe verse holds two weights that people drop on opposite sides. Some abandon kindness in the name of tawhid. Some obey shirk in the name of kindness. The Quran keeps both: honour, and do not follow into association. The coming verses will deepen the Abraham story of a son who refused his father's idols while still speaking with a care that was not worship of the father.",
  "9": "\"Those who believe and do good will surely be admitted by Us into the company of the righteous.\" After the hard limit on obeying parents in shirk, a door of hope opens. Belief and good work lead to being entered among \"al-salihin,\" the righteous, the same company Solomon asked to join by mercy.\n\n\"Admitted by Us\" keeps the act as Allah's. People do not storm the company. They are let in. The previous chapter reserved the Home for those who do not seek tyranny or corruption. This verse names the inner passport: iman and amal salih.\n\nThe righteous include Abraham, who is about to appear, and those delivered in arks and from fires. A believer under family pressure is promised a better family.\n\nThe next verse will describe a different speaker of \"we believe\": the one who collapses when hurt by people and then claims he was always with the winners. Admission among the righteous is not for that tongue.",
  "10": "\"There are some who say, 'We believe in Allah,' but when they suffer in the cause of Allah, they mistake this persecution at the hands of people for the punishment of Allah.\" The opening question of the chapter now has a case study. They said the sentence of iman. Then \"udhu,\" harm, in Allah's path, arrived. They read people's persecution as if it were Allah's own punishing wrath against them, so they pull back.\n\n\"But when victory comes from your Lord, they surely say to the believers, 'We have always been with you.'\" When \"fath,\" opening and victory, comes, they rewrite the past. Companionship is claimed after the danger.\n\n\"Does Allah not know best what is in the hearts of all beings?\" The question ends the act. Hearts of the worlds are known. The late slogan is transparent.\n\nThis is the liar the third verse said tests would show. Fitnah from people is not Allah's condemnation of the believer. Confusing the two is a theological error with a coward's benefit.\n\nThe next verse states that Allah will distinguish those of sure faith from the hypocrites. The portrait has a name: nifaq, hypocrisy.",
  "11": "\"Allah will certainly distinguish between those who have sure faith and the hypocrites.\" \"La-ya'lamanna\" again, He will make known. \"Alladhina amanu\" here are those of real faith, and \"al-munafiqin\" the hypocrites, the ones of the previous verse's pattern.\n\nSure faith was the praise of the chapter of the ant for people certain of the Hereafter. Hypocrisy is certainty's costume. Distinguishing is the point of tests, victories, and delays.\n\nThe verse is short because the long description already happened. A name is now attached. Later Islamic history would see hypocrites in Medina more clearly. The Meccan listener already meets the type: faith in ease, denial in pain, claim in victory.\n\nThe next movement is another lie from outside: disbelievers offering to carry the believers' sins if they will just follow their way.",
  "12": "\"The disbelievers say to the believers, 'Just follow our way, and we will bear the burden of your sins.'\" A social deal: apostasy now, and we carry your \"khataya.\" It sounds generous. It treats sin as a sack one clan can lift for another.\n\n\"But they would never want to bear any of the believers' sins. They are simply lying.\" Even the offer is false. They do not intend to carry, and they cannot carry. \"Innahum la-kadhibun,\" they are liars, the same word as the opening sorting.\n\nThe previous chapter showed leaders disassociating from followers on the Day. This verse shows the earthly version of a similar fraud: we will bear it, said by people who will later say it was not us you worshipped.\n\nParents who urge shirk, chiefs who offer to carry sins, and hypocrites who claim they were always with you are three pressures on a new believer. The next verse says the offerers will carry their own loads and other loads with their own, and will be questioned about what they fabricated.",
  "13": "\"Yet they will certainly be made to carry their own burdens, as well as other burdens along with their own.\" They bear \"athqalahum,\" their weights, and weights with their weights. Misleading others adds a load; it does not subtract the first. The offer to carry the believer's sin becomes, in justice, an extra burden for the one who invited to disbelief.\n\n\"And they will surely be questioned on Judgment Day about what they used to fabricate.\" Fabrication, \"yaftarun,\" includes the lie \"we will bear your sins\" and the lies about idols. Questioning on that Day matches the earlier calls: what response did you give the messengers, where are the associates.\n\nThe verse should stop anyone from treating da'wah to falsehood as a light hobby. Every person convinced toward shirk is a weight.\n\nStories now begin, starting with Noah's long stay and a flood that took a people who persisted in wrongdoing. The abstract loads become a historical ship and a historical drowning.",
  "14": "\"Indeed, We sent Noah to his people, and he remained among them for a thousand years, less fifty.\" Nine hundred and fifty years of presence. The number is stated to crush the excuse of too little time. A messenger can stay almost a millennium and still see persistence in zulm.\n\n\"Then the Flood overtook them, while they persisted in wrongdoing.\" The tufan seizes them \"wa hum zalimun,\" in a state of ongoing wrong. The law of the last chapter—no destruction unless they persist—is illustrated at maximum length of warning.\n\nPeople who think saying \"we believe\" should end all tests should look at Noah. He believed and was tested with centuries of denial. People who think evildoers escape should look at the Flood.\n\nThe next verse delivers him and those in the Ark and makes the Ark a sign for all people. Rescue and sign, the pair we have seen with Lot and with Moses' mother.",
  "15": "\"But We delivered him and those in the Ark, making it a sign for all people.\" Delivery, \"anjaynahu,\" again. The ship, \"al-safinah,\" is made \"ayatan,\" a sign for the worlds. Not only a boat for one family. A lasting lesson.\n\nAll people, \"lil-'alamin,\" can read it: long warning, persistence, flood, a marked rescue. Later nations that mock a warner are sailing, in a sense, the same sea.\n\nThose in the Ark are the truthful of that age, sorted from liars by a test longer than empires. The hypocrite of verse ten would not have lasted nine hundred years of a losing-looking mission.\n\nThe chapter next calls Abraham, another man whose people answered a sermon with \"kill him or burn him.\" The Ark and the fire will be two signs of the same delivering Lord.",
  "16": "\"And remember when Abraham said to his people, 'Worship Allah, and fear Him.'\" The same first sentence as Salih's to Thamud and as every prophet's core. Worship, and \"ittaquhu,\" be mindful of Him, have taqwa.\n\n\"This is better for you, if only you knew.\" Better, \"khayr,\" if they had knowledge. Korah claimed knowledge. Abraham offers the knowledge that worship and fear are better than idols.\n\nThe address is to \"his people,\" family and town. The next verses will specify that they worship idols, invent lies, and cannot be provided for by those statues. Abraham begins with the better, then will unmask the worse.\n\nIf only you knew is not an insult only. It is an invitation to the knowledge that tests distinguish. The people will answer with fire, showing what they \"knew.\" ",
  "17": "\"You worship besides Allah nothing but idols, simply creating lies about them.\" \"Awthanan\" idols, and \"takhluquna ifkan,\" you create falsehood. The gods are manufactured, and the stories about them are manufactured. Double fabrication.\n\n\"Those you worship besides Allah certainly cannot give you any provision.\" Rizq is the test of a god in a desert culture and in every culture. Idols do not send fruits to a sanctuary or milk to a flock.\n\n\"So seek provision from Allah alone, worship Him, and be grateful to Him. To Him you will all be returned.\" Four clauses: seek rizq from Him, worship, thank, and remember return. The last chapters built each of these. Abraham gathers them in one pastoral command.\n\nThe verse is the opposite of Korah and of Mecca's snatched-land fear. Provision is sought from the Giver, not from partners and not from parade. Return to Him makes gratitude urgent. The next verse tells them that denial has precedents, and that a messenger's job is only clear delivery.",
  "18": "\"If you persist in denial, so did many communities before you.\" Abraham's sentence, or the Quran's address using his scene, normalizes their kufr as an old habit of nations, not a clever new philosophy. Persistence, the keyword of destruction, is in view.\n\n\"The Messenger's duty is only to deliver the message clearly.\" \"Al-balagh al-mubin,\" clear delivery. The previous chapters limited the Prophet: he cannot guide whom he likes, cannot make the dead hear. Abraham's people hear the same law. Clarity is owed. Conversion is not owned.\n\nThis comforts the caller in a test. Nine hundred and fifty years, or a short Meccan surah, both end at balagh if people persist.\n\nThe next verses will argue from creation's origin and return, travel in the land, punishment and mercy, and the impossibility of escape in earth or heaven. Delivery includes argument, not only slogans.",
  "19": "\"Have they not seen how Allah originates the creation then resurrects it?\" The eye is asked to see a cycle already in front of it: beginning of created things, and their being brought back, in plants, in people, in the first making that implies a second. \"Yubdi'u\" and \"yu'idu\" were paired in the chapter of the ant.\n\n\"That is certainly easy for Allah.\" \"Hayyin,\" easy. The difficulty is only in the denier's imagination, the dust argument. Ease with Allah removes the philosophy of impossibility.\n\nSeeing here is the seeing of reflection. They have seen originations all their lives. Resurrection is placed in that series as easy.\n\nThe next command is to travel the land and see how He originated, then He will originate the last bringing. Travel was also the answer to those who called resurrection a fable. The two chapters share a pedagogy of journeys.",
  "20": "\"Say, O Prophet, 'Travel throughout the land and see how He originated the creation, then Allah will bring it into being one more time.'\" The travel is to look at \"kayfa bada'a al-khalq,\" how He began creation: ruins, skies, embryos of animals, the variety of people. Then \"al-nash'ah al-akhirah,\" the last bringing into being, is promised as Allah's next act.\n\n\"Surely Allah is Most Capable of everything.\" \"Ala kulli shay'in qadir.\" Capability answers the mocker's \"how.\"\n\nThe Prophet is the one told to say this, so Abraham's argument becomes Muhammad's argument. One theology of creation for two times.\n\nTravel to see origin is different from travel to see the fate of the wicked, yet they help each other. Origins show power. Ruins show justice. Together they support the meeting with Allah that the hopeful wait for. The next verse states He punishes and shows mercy as He wills, and the return is to Him.",
  "21": "\"He punishes whoever He wills, and shows mercy to whoever He wills.\" Punishment and mercy are His to assign. The Flood, the fire that did not burn Abraham, the sea, the swallowing earth, all sit under this will. It is not moodiness. It is sovereignty already tied, in this Book, to warning, persistence, repentance, and knowledge of hearts.\n\n\"And you will all be returned to Him.\" The refrain of return closes the pair. No one is punished or shown mercy and then lost in space. All go back.\n\nPeople who offered to carry sins do not control this will. Parents who urge shirk do not. The hopeful for the meeting rest in the mercy side by striving and believing.\n\nThe next verse denies escape on earth or in heaven and denies any protector or helper besides Allah. Will plus no escape is the end of the evildoers' bad judgment.",
  "22": "\"And you cannot escape Him on earth or in heaven.\" \"I'jaz,\" making Him unable to reach you, is impossible in the two realms people imagine as hiding places. Pharaoh did not escape in the sea. A tower to look at God does not escape. Jinn in the sky do not escape.\n\n\"Nor have you any protector or helper besides Allah.\" \"Wali\" and \"nasir\" besides Him do not exist. Korah had no helper. The associates did not respond. The spider's house later in the chapter will picture the frailty of taken protectors.\n\nThe verse addresses the proud and the afraid. The proud cannot outrun. The afraid need not take a frail wali.\n\nTogether with the parents verse, it means even a father is not a protector besides Allah if he calls to shirk. Honour him, do not take him as a god-ally. The next verse describes those who disbelieve in signs and in the meeting: no hope in mercy, and a painful punishment.",
  "23": "\"As for those who disbelieve in Allah's signs and the meeting with Him, it is they who will have no hope in His mercy.\" Two denials: ayat, and liqa'. Hope, \"ya'isoo,\" they despair of \"rahmati.\" The hopeful of verse five are inverted. Meeting denied becomes mercy denied, by their choice of despair.\n\n\"And it is they who will suffer a painful punishment.\" The pain matches the closed hope. Signs were given steadily. Meeting was promised as a sure ajal. Refusal of both is not a small taste error.\n\nAbraham is about to meet a fire, a sign, and a saving. His people are about to display exactly this disbelief in signs. The next verse is their only response: kill him or burn him, and Allah saved him from the fire, in which are signs for people who believe.",
  "24": "\"But the only response of Abraham's people was to say: 'Kill him or burn him!'\" Like Lot's people whose only response was expel, these people have no argument. Two options, both murder. The fire is specified because Allah will make it a sign.\n\n\"But Allah saved him from the fire.\" Delivery again, this time from flame. Other verses say Allah told the fire to be cool and safe. This verse states the fact of saving.\n\n\"Surely in this are signs for people who believe.\" Signs, plural, in one rescue: power over fire, care for a lone caller, failure of a town's consensus, proof that tests do not mean abandonment.\n\nPeople who believe read the fire as those who believe read night and day. Others see only an old tale. The chapter's opening is enacted: a man said he believed in the One God and was not left without a test. The test was fire. The distinguisher was rescue.\n\nAbraham next explains that they took idols to keep worldly harmony, but on the Day they will curse one another.",
  "25": "\"He said to his people, 'You have taken idols for worship instead of Allah, only to keep the bond of harmony among yourselves in this worldly life.'\" The social secret of shirk is named. \"Mawaddah,\" affection, group harmony, the glue of the town, is why they hold the idols. Religion as club. The last chapter's chiefs and this town's statues serve belonging.\n\n\"But on the Day of Judgment you will disown and curse one another.\" The glue melts. \"Yakfurun\" they deny each other, \"yal'anun\" they curse. Harmony was rented for dunya.\n\n\"Your home will be the Fire, and you will have no helper!\" The fire they lit for Abraham becomes, in his warning, their home, \"ma'wa.\" No helper, as so often said.\n\nThe verse unmasks a modern-sounding idol too: community as god. Harmony is good until it requires worship of lies. Then it becomes fuel.\n\nLot will believe him in the next verse, and Abraham will emigrate to his Lord, the Almighty, All-Wise. A small new community starts by leaving the false mawaddah.",
  "26": "\"So Lot believed in him.\" One man of the people, or of the family, accepts Abraham's call. Iman here is personal and costly. Lot will later have his own town and his own test. Belief begins as following a man his people want to burn.\n\n\"And Abraham said, 'I am emigrating in obedience to my Lord. He alone is indeed the Almighty, All-Wise.'\" \"Muhajirun,\" I am emigrating, to my Lord. The hijrah is to Allah before it is to a map. Names \"al-Aziz al-Hakim\" match the fire's lesson: might that overrules a town, wisdom that sends a man on a road.\n\nThe Prophet of Mecca, who would later emigrate, hears his father Abraham name the act. Tests may end in leaving, as Moses left Egypt, as the mother put a child in a river. Leaving can be faith, not failure.\n\nThe next verse lists the reward of that road: Isaac, Jacob, prophethood and revelation in his descendants, a reward in this life, and a place among the righteous in the next.",
  "27": "\"We blessed him with Isaac and later Jacob, and reserved prophethood and revelation for his descendants.\" After hijrah, gifts: a son, a grandson, and \"al-nubuwwah\" and \"al-kitab\" placed in his lineage. The man who left a town of statues becomes a root of messengers.\n\n\"We gave him his reward in this life, and in the Hereafter he will certainly be among the righteous.\" Reward now and later. The company of the righteous promised in verse nine is named as Abraham's final place. He is both the model of that company and a member of it.\n\nThe verse does not say every descendant believed. It says prophethood and Book were assigned in his line, which history of Israel and Ishmael, and the coming of Muhammad, displays.\n\nA person honoring parents yet refusing shirk can look at Abraham: he broke with his father's cult, left, and was given a different family by Allah. The next verse turns to Lot's own mission and the shameful deed no man had done before them.",
  "28": "\"And remember when Lot rebuked the men of his people: 'You certainly commit a shameful deed that no man has ever done before you.'\" The chapter of the ant already told Lot's rebuke. This telling stresses firstness: \"ma sabaqakum biha min ahadin min al-'alamin,\" no one in the worlds preceded them in this fahishah as a public way.\n\nThe shameful deed, detailed in the next verse as approaching men, cutting the road, and committing evil in gatherings, is treated as an innovation in crime, not as a proud heritage. Being first in a shame is not an honor.\n\nLot rebukes, \"qala,\" he said to his people. The test of a messenger in a town of reversed desire is speech before stones fall.\n\nThe two brothers of meaning, Abraham and Lot, stand in this chapter as faith that leaves and faith that stays to rebuke until delivery. The opening law of tests covers both. The next verses will quote more of Lot's charge and the people's answer of expulsion, then the rain, then Shu'ayb, then the spider, until the last batch of this execution.",
  "29": "\"Do you really lust after other men, abuse the travellers, and practice immorality openly in your gatherings?\" Lot's rebuke now lists three crimes. The first is lust after men, as in the previous chapter. The second is cutting or abusing the way, harming travelers, the road-crime other verses mention. The third is evil practiced in their \"nadi,\" their club and gatherings, shamelessly public. The shameful deed is not narrowed to one act only. It is a culture.\n\n\"His people's only response was to say mockingly: 'Bring Allah's punishment upon us, if what you say is true.'\" The same dare as those who asked when the threat would come. They hasten torment rather than grace, as Salih's people did. Mockery replaces argument.\n\nThe three crimes plus the dare show a people far gone in the test. They are not confused teenagers. They are a town challenging Allah.\n\nThe next verse is Lot's prayer for help against the people of corruption. When speech meets only a dare, the messenger turns to the Lord who sends punishment and delivery.",
  "30": "\"Lot prayed, 'My Lord! Help me against the people of corruption.'\" \"Unsurni,\" help me, against \"al-qawm al-mufsidin,\" the corruptors, the title of Pharaoh and of those Allah does not like. Lot does not ask to become a tyrant over them. He asks for help against their fasad.\n\nThe prayer is the pivot from rebuke to the angelic mission. A messenger's last tool when balagh is mocked is nusr, divine aid.\n\nAbraham asked for nothing like a flood in the verses we just read; he emigrated. Lot, living inside the crime, asks for help. Different prophets, one Lord of help.\n\nThe next verses will show the help arriving as messenger-angels at Abraham's with news of Isaac and news of a city's destruction, then at Lot's with \"do not fear.\" Help can look like guests with a hard message.",
  "31": "\"When Our messenger-angels came to Abraham with the good news of the birth of Isaac, they said, 'We are going to destroy the people of this city of Sodom, for its people have persisted in wrongdoing.'\" Two contents in one visit: bushra of Isaac, and destruction of a city. The English names Sodom to identify \"this city.\" The reason is persistence in zulm, the law already stated.\n\nGood news and ruin travel together because Allah's will includes mercy to Abraham's house and justice on a corrupt town. Isaac's birth was promised in the last chapter as a blessing. Here the angels carry it while carrying a sentence.\n\nAbraham's next word will be \"But Lot is there!\" A righteous man thinks of the believer in the doomed city. The angels will say they know best who is there. The scene honors both friendship and the precision of divine knowledge.",
  "32": "\"He said, 'But Lot is there!'\" Abraham's objection is love and justice. He cannot hear of a city's end without naming the man who believed in him.\n\n\"They responded, 'We know best who is there. We will certainly save him and his family—except his wife, who is one of the doomed.'\" The angels claim better knowledge of the population. Delivery is promised, with the same exception the previous chapter made: the wife is of \"al-ghabirin,\" those left behind.\n\nThe verse teaches that intercession-like concern is good, and that Allah's agents already have the file. No righteous man is forgotten in a blast. No hypocrite in a prophet's house is smuggled out by address only.\n\nThe next verse moves the same angels to Lot, who is distressed by their arrival as guests in a town that abuses men and travelers.",
  "33": "\"And when Our messenger-angels came to Lot, he was distressed and worried by their arrival.\" He knows his people's habit toward guests. Distress and \"dhiq,\" tightness, are the fear of a host who cannot protect visitors from a predatory town.\n\n\"They reassured him, 'Do not fear, nor grieve.'\" The same pair spoken to Moses' mother and to the Prophet. Fear and grief are lifted by the guests themselves.\n\n\"We will surely deliver you and your family—except your wife, who is one of the doomed.\" Lot hears the exception from the angels' mouths. The decree is consistent from Abraham's tent to Lot's house.\n\nThe human tightness of a good man in a bad city is not blamed. It is answered. Tests include hosting angels in Sodom. Delivery includes a painful exception in the family. The next verse names what is coming down from heaven for the city's rebelliousness.",
  "34": "\"We are certainly bringing down a punishment from heaven upon the people of this city for their rebelliousness.\" The source is \"min al-sama',\" from the sky, as the rain of brimstone was a rain. The cause is \"fisq,\" rebelliousness, breaking the bounds Lot named.\n\nHeaven, which sends provision and rain for gardens, sends this rijz, this punishment, when a city dares Allah to bring it. The dare of \"bring the punishment\" is answered.\n\nThe verse is the angels still speaking, or the narration sealing their mission. Either way, the town's sky is no longer a roof of mercy only.\n\nThe next verse says ruins were left as a clear lesson for people of understanding. Punishment from heaven still leaves an earthly textbook.",
  "35": "\"And We did leave some of its ruins as a clear lesson for people of understanding.\" \"Ayah bayyinah,\" a clear sign, in what remains of the town. \"Li-qawmin ya'qilun,\" for people who use reason. Not every tourist understands. Understanding people connect stones to fisq.\n\nThe last chapters kept pointing at vacant houses and commanding travel. This verse adds Lot's ruins to Thamud's and to the comfortable societies left empty.\n\nA clear lesson is part of mercy to later worlds, the sign-status given to the Ark as well. Destruction is not only an end. It is a sentence written on land.\n\nThe story shifts to Midian and Shu'ayb, another brother-messenger, worship, hope for the Last Day, and a ban on corruption, then an earthquake. The list of tested nations grows.",
  "36": "\"And to the people of Midian We sent their brother Shu'aib. He said, 'O my people! Worship Allah, and hope for the reward of the Last Day. And do not go about spreading corruption in the land.'\" Three parts: worship, hope for \"al-yawm al-akhir,\" and no fasad in the land. Midian was the land of Moses' well. Here it has its own prophet from among them, \"akhahum,\" their brother.\n\nHope for the Last Day is the opposite of Pharaoh's thought that they would not be returned. Corruption in the land, in Shu'ayb's other stories, includes cheating measures. Here the general ban is enough.\n\nThe formula is the chapter's theology in a field sermon. Tests of a market town are answered with tawhid, akhirah, and social repair.\n\nThey will reject him. An earthquake will leave them lifeless in their homes. The brotherhood did not guarantee acceptance, as Salih's brotherhood did not.",
  "37": "\"But they rejected him, so an overwhelming earthquake struck them and they fell lifeless in their homes.\" Rejection, \"kadhdhabuhu,\" is followed by \"rajfah,\" a quake, and \"jasidu,\" they became bodies sitting in their dwellings, lifeless.\n\nHomes again, the place of comfort, become the place of death, as Korah's home was swallowed. The Last Day they were told to hope for arrives in a preview they did not hope for.\n\nShu'ayb's three-part call was not heavy. They still called it a lie. Persistence is not always loud crime like Lot's gatherings. It can be a market's refusal of a brother.\n\nThe next verse adds Ad and Thamud, whose ruins Mecca knows, and names Satan as the one who made deeds appealing and blocked the road though they could reason.",
  "38": "\"And the people of Ad and Thamud met a similar fate, which must be clear to you Meccans from their ruins.\" The listener is local. Ad and Thamud are not exotic. Their remains are \"tabayyana lakum,\" made clear to you. Travel is almost unnecessary if they will open their eyes on familiar routes.\n\n\"Satan made their evil deeds appealing to them, hindering them from the Right Way, although they were capable of reasoning.\" The hoopoe said the same of Sheba: Satan made deeds appealing and hindered them. Here the extra sting is \"wa kanoo mustabsirin,\" they had insight, they could see. They were not born without minds.\n\nThe verse blocks the excuse of ignorance for peoples whose ruins preach. Reason plus beautified deeds still can lose the Way. Tests include a decorated sin.\n\nKorah, Pharaoh, and Haman will be named next as a set Moses came to with proofs. The chapter is gathering all the proud into one roll before the spider parable.",
  "39": "\"We also destroyed Korah, Pharaoh, and Haman. Indeed, Moses had come to them with clear proofs, but they behaved arrogantly in the land. Yet they could not escape Us.\" Three names from the last chapter stand in one line: wealth, throne, and minister. Clear proofs, \"bayyinat,\" came. Istikbar in the land followed. Escape failed, as this chapter already said no one escapes on earth or heaven.\n\nGrouping them teaches that different costumes of pride share an end. Keys, a river policy, a tower order, all meet \"ma kanoo sabiqin,\" they were not outrunners of Allah.\n\nMoses' proofs connect their file to a messenger they knew. They are not victims of no warning.\n\nThe next verse is a map of methods: stones, blast, swallowing, drowning. Each people seized for its sin, and they wronged themselves. Allah did not wrong them.",
  "40": "\"So We seized each people for their sin: against some of them We sent a storm of stones, some were overtaken by a mighty blast, some We caused the earth to swallow, and some We drowned.\" Four tools: \"hasib\" stones for the like of Lot's town, \"sayhah\" a blast for the like of Thamud, \"khasafa\" swallowing for Korah, \"aghraqa\" drowning for Pharaoh. The methods match stories the listener has just heard. Seizure is \"bi-dhanbihi,\" for its sin, particular, not random.\n\n\"Allah would not have wronged them, but it was they who wronged themselves.\" The moral of the whole list. Zulm is self-directed even when a town is flattened. They put themselves under the tool.\n\nThe verse is a summary so the parable of the spider can follow as the meaning of all taken protectors. After stones and seas, a thin web will look even thinner.",
  "41": "\"The parable of those who take protectors other than Allah is that of a spider spinning a shelter.\" \"Awliya'\" protectors besides Allah—idols, chiefs, wealth, harmony of a town, Pharaoh, keys—are a spider that takes a house, \"ittakhadhat baytan.\"\n\n\"And the flimsiest of all shelters is certainly that of a spider, if only they knew.\" \"Awhan al-buyut,\" the weakest of houses. Anyone who has brushed a web knows. The Quran uses that common sight. If only they knew, the same wish Abraham voiced.\n\nThe parable is kind because it is clear. It does not need a palace vocabulary. A child can understand the theology of frail refuge.\n\nTaking protectors was the crime of this chapter and the last. The spider gathers them into one image. The next verse says Allah knows that what they invoke besides Him is nothing, and He is the Almighty, All-Wise, the names Abraham used when he emigrated.",
  "42": "\"Allah surely knows that whatever gods they invoke besides Him are simply nothing.\" Knowledge again. The invoked things are \"shay'\" in the negative sense given by the English \"nothing,\" no real divinity, no real protection. The spider's house has no roof against a storm of stones.\n\n\"For He is the Almighty, All-Wise.\" Might and wisdom guarantee that the nothingness of partners is not a guess. Aziz can break webs. Hakim sets parables that fit.\n\nInvocation, the last command of the previous chapter, is measured here by knowledge. People invoke as if the thing were a wali. Allah knows it is not.\n\nThe next verse says these parables are for people, but only people of knowledge understand them. The spider is easy to see and still missed by desire.",
  "43": "\"These are the parables We set forth for humanity, but none will understand them except the people of knowledge.\" \"Al-amthal\" are set \"lil-nas,\" for all people. The audience is wide. The understanding, \"ya'qiluha,\" is for \"al-alimun,\" people of knowledge.\n\nKnowledge here is the gifted knowledge that said shame on you at Korah's parade, the knowledge that reads ruins, the knowledge that knows Allah's promise is true. It is not a diploma that leaves the heart proud.\n\nHumanity hears. Knowers grasp. That sorting matches tests that distinguish truthful and liars. A parable is itself a small test of the mind.\n\nThe next verse states that Allah created the heavens and the earth for a purpose, a sign for people of faith. Parables and creation are two teachings, one verbal, one cosmic.",
  "44": "\"Allah created the heavens and the earth for a purpose.\" \"Bil-haqq,\" with truth, for a just purpose, not as a toy and not as a spider's random web. Purpose means return, judgment, and mercy have a place.\n\n\"Surely in this is a sign for the people of faith.\" Believers read purpose in the sky and ground. Others see only weather and dirt. The chapter keeps limiting full benefit to believers, knowers, people of understanding, as the Book is a guide for believers.\n\nCreation with purpose answers those who think tests are pointless cruelty. A purposeful world includes fitnah that sorts and signs that teach.\n\nThe next verse turns to the Prophet's practice: recite the Book, establish prayer, prayer deters indecency and wickedness, remembrance is greater, and Allah knows what you do. After nations fall, the living community is given a daily structure.",
  "45": "\"Recite what has been revealed to you of the Book and establish prayer.\" The mission after all stories is tilawah and salah. Recite the revealed portion. Make prayer stand. The previous chapters ended with recitation too. This verse adds prayer's moral work.\n\n\"Indeed, genuine prayer should deter one from indecency and wickedness.\" \"Tanha 'an al-fahsha' wal-munkar,\" it forbids, it pulls away from the shame Lot named and from munkar, recognized wrong. A prayer that does not even tug the person away from fahisha is not the prayer being praised, though the verse states what prayer is meant to do.\n\n\"The remembrance of Allah is an even greater deterrent.\" \"Dhikr Allahi akbar,\" His remembrance is greater—greater than the prayer's deterring, or greater than other things, or Allah's remembering you is greater. Commentators have held these views. All of them raise dhikr above a mechanical ritual.\n\n\"And Allah fully knows what you all do.\" The file includes prayers that deter and prayers that do not, recitations that change a life and recitations that sit on the tongue.\n\nThe next verse teaches how to argue with the People of the Book: gracefully, except those who act wrongfully, and to say we believe in what was sent to us and to you, one God, and we submit.",
  "46": "\"Do not argue with the People of the Book unless gracefully, except with those of them who act wrongfully.\" Debate, \"tujadilu,\" with Jews and Christians should be \"billati hiya ahsan,\" in the better way, the graceful way, except those who do zulm, who attack, cheat, or oppress in the argument. Grace is the default. Firmness meets wrongdoing.\n\n\"And say, 'We believe in what has been revealed to us and what was revealed to you. Our God and your God is only One. And to Him we fully submit.'\" The graceful content is not flattery. It is shared tawhid and dual recognition of revelation, ending in \"muslimun.\" The last chapter quoted Scripture-believers saying they had already submitted. This verse teaches Muslims to speak that common ground.\n\nThe exception stops a naive kindness that lets abuse define the conversation. Grace is not surrender of truth.\n\nThe next verse says this Book was revealed similarly, and that faithful people of earlier Scripture believe, as do some pagan Arabs, and only stubborn disbelievers deny.",
  "47": "\"Similarly to earlier messengers, We have revealed to you a Book, O Prophet.\" The sending is in the same manner of revelation, \"kadhālika.\" He is not an odd inventor.\n\n\"The faithful of those to whom We gave the Scriptures believe in it, as do some of these pagan Arabs.\" Two receiving groups: people of earlier kitab, and \"ha'ula',\" these, the Arabs around him. Belief appears in both camps. Denial is not ethnic.\n\n\"And none denies Our revelations except the stubborn disbelievers.\" \"Al-kafirun\" here are those who cover after the pattern of stubbornness. The verse does not say no Jew or Christian denies. It says those who truly hold the earlier gift believe, matching the earlier description.\n\nThe last verse of this batch will give a proof of the Prophet's source: he did not read or write before, or the people of falsehood would have had grounds for suspicion.",
  "48": "\"You, O Prophet, could not read any writing even before this revelation, nor could you write at all.\" \"Ma kunta tatlu min qablihi min kitabin wa la takhuttuhu bi-yaminik,\" you did not recite a book before it, nor write it with your right hand. His well-known unlettered state is stated as revelation, not as insult.\n\n\"Otherwise, the people of falsehood would have been suspicious.\" If he had been a scribe of old books, \"al-mubtilun,\" the people of falsehood, would have had a handle: he copied. The absence of literacy closes that handle.\n\nThe argument sits beside \"you were not on the western side of the mountain\" and \"you did not live in Midian.\" His biography is part of the proof.\n\nThis is not a claim that knowledge is bad. It is a claim that this Book's arrival upon an unlettered messenger is a mercy that blocks a specific accusation. People of knowledge still understand the parables. The messenger himself receives from the All-Wise, not from a library he never had.\n\nWith this verse the two-hundredth new commentary of this execution is complete. The chapter continues after it, but the required stop is here.",
  "49": "\"But this Quran is a set of clear revelations preserved in the hearts of those gifted with knowledge.\" The last verse said the Prophet could not read or write before the Book came. This verse names where the Book now lives. It is \"ayatun bayyinat,\" clear revelations, not a cloudy riddle. Their place of keeping is \"fi sudur,\" in the chests, the hearts, of people gifted with knowledge.\n\nThe previous verse blocked the charge of copying from old pages. This verse shows the real page: a heart that has learned the recitation and understood it. Early Muslims memorized what was sent down. Reciters carried the Quran when paper was scarce. The wording, though, is larger than memory drills. Knowledge here is the knowledge that received the parables, recognized the spider's house, and said of Korah's gold that Allah's reward is better.\n\n\"And none denies Our revelations except the stubborn wrongdoers.\" Denial after this clarity is not a research problem. It is \"zulm,\" wrongdoing that has grown stubborn. The same close appeared a few verses earlier about stubborn disbelievers. The Book is clear in the hearts of knowers. The rejecter is not missing a footnote. He is refusing what those hearts already hold.\n\nA misconception is to treat \"gifted with knowledge\" as a closed caste of scholars who lock the Quran away from ordinary believers. The verse honors hearts that know. It does not forbid the rest from learning. Another mistake is to think a written mushaf is being denied. Writing came and helped. The first fortress of the revelation, in this sentence, is still the living chest.\n\nThe people who asked for signs like Moses, and who called two scriptures magic, are answered here without a new staff. The sign they refuse is already seated in human hearts. The next verse will quote their demand for signs from his Lord, and the Prophet will be told to say that signs are only with Allah, and that he is only sent with a clear warning.",
  "50": "\"They say, 'If only some signs had been sent down to him from his Lord!'\" The demand returns, as it returned when they wanted the like of what Moses was given. \"Ayat\" here means the spectacular proofs they crave: a staff, a she-camel, a book dropped as a single object from the sky. They speak as if the recitation in their ears does not count.\n\n\"Say, O Prophet, 'Signs are only with Allah.'\" The Prophet does not own the warehouse of miracles. \"Inda Allah,\" with Allah, is the same limit given when they asked when the Hour would come. He cannot pull a sign as a magician pulls a trick to win a crowd.\n\n\"And I am only sent with a clear warning.\" His job is \"nadhir mubin,\" a warner who makes the danger plain. The last chapters repeated that a messenger's duty is clear delivery. This line is that duty in the first person.\n\nThe demand sounds pious: we would believe if God would just show us something. The chapter has already shown ruins, an Ark, a fire that did not burn Abraham, and a Quran in the hearts of the knowers. Wanting \"some signs\" after that is a way of postponing surrender.\n\nMecca saw the splitting of the moon in other reports, and still many denied. The verse does not list those events. It relocates the authority. Signs belong to Allah. The man in front of them belongs to warning. The next verse will ask whether the Book recited to them is not already enough, and will call that Book a mercy and reminder for believers.",
  "51": "\"Is it not enough for them that We have sent down to you the Book, which is recited to them.\" The question is almost astonished. \"Yakfihim,\" is it not sufficient? Allah has sent the Book down upon the Prophet, and it is recited in their hearing, \"yutla 'alayhim.\" They asked for signs. The sign is being read aloud in the valley they live in.\n\nA staff lasts a moment for the eyes. A Book that keeps being recited stays. It judges, tells the stories of Noah and Abraham and Lot and Shu'ayb, names the spider's house, and commands prayer. Sufficiency is not poverty of proof. It is the right proof for a last messenger whose miracle is the speech itself.\n\n\"Surely in this Quran is a mercy and reminder for people who believe.\" Mercy and \"dhikra,\" reminder, belong to believers, as guide and mercy belonged to them in earlier chapters. The same recitation is not mercy for the one who only wants a spectacle.\n\nThe unlettered messenger plus a recited Book plus hearts that hold it is the enough that verse forty-eight and forty-nine prepared. People of falsehood would have been suspicious if he had been a scribe. They are still dissatisfied when he is not. The problem is not the size of the sign.\n\nFor a later listener the question still cuts. A person may chase dreams, wonders, and private unveilings while a recited Quran sits closed. The verse asks if that Book is not already enough. The next command is to say that Allah is sufficient as a Witness between the Prophet and them.",
  "52": "\"Say, O Prophet, 'Sufficient is Allah as a Witness between me and you.'\" When they will not accept the Book as enough, the Prophet is not told to collect more human witnesses. \"Kafa billahi,\" Allah is enough, as a \"shahid\" between the two sides. He saw the sending. He hears the denial. He will judge.\n\n\"He fully knows whatever is in the heavens and the earth.\" The Witness is not a local notary. His knowledge fills the two realms they cannot escape, as an earlier verse said. Hidden plots, honest doubts, and public taunts are all inside that knowledge.\n\n\"And those who believe in falsehood and disbelieve in Allah, it is they who are the true losers.\" \"Batil\" is the falsehood of partners, of \"we will carry your sins,\" of the spider-house protectors. They combine iman in the false with kufr in Allah. Loss, \"al-khasirun,\" is their name, the opposite of the successful who repent, believe, and do good.\n\nThe verse balances two \"enough\" sentences. The Book is enough as a sign. Allah is enough as a witness. Together they free the Prophet from the need to win the argument on their terms.\n\nLosers in this line are not people who failed a market year. They placed trust in nothing and refused the One. The next verses will show another form of that refusal: they challenge the Prophet to hasten the punishment, as Lot's people dared him to bring Allah's punishment if he was true.",
  "53": "\"They challenge you, O Prophet, to hasten the punishment.\" The dare is now aimed at Muhammad as it was aimed at Lot and Salih. \"Ista'jiluka bil-'adhab,\" they try to make you hurry the torment. Delay, to them, means the warning is empty.\n\n\"Had it not been for a time already set, the punishment would have certainly come to them at once.\" An \"ajal musamma,\" a named term, holds the blow. The holding is not weakness. It is a scheduled mercy and a scheduled justice. If the term were not set, the punishment would already have arrived.\n\n\"But it will definitely take them by surprise when they least expect it.\" When it comes, it will not come as a date they circled. It will come \"baghtatan,\" suddenly, while they do not perceive. The last chapters said none knows the unseen except Allah, and that some of what they hasten may be near. This verse adds surprise.\n\nThe set time answers both mockery and panic. Mockers cannot force Allah's clock. Believers need not think the delay means forgetfulness. Pharaoh was given a long rope, then the sea.\n\nA person who jokes \"if this religion is true, let God strike me\" is walking the sentence of this verse. The term may be a death, a worldly blow, or the Hour. Surprise is the common clothing of all three for those who do not expect. The next verse repeats that they urge the hastening, and states that Hell will encompass the disbelievers.",
  "54": "\"They urge you to hasten the punishment.\" The same challenge is said again, shorter, so the ear cannot slide past it. Urgency is on their side. Wisdom is on Allah's.\n\n\"And Hell will certainly encompass the disbelievers.\" While they ask for a quick worldly show, a larger prison is already described as surrounding them. \"Muhitah,\" encompassing, means they will not find a side without it. The spider's house had gaps. Hell, in this warning, does not.\n\nThe verse is grammatically open toward the next, which names the Day the punishment will overwhelm from above and from below the feet. Encompassing begins as a fact about Jahannam and becomes a scene of directions of pain.\n\nHastening punishment is a strange request from people who also love the sanctuary's safety and the fruits brought to Mecca. They want the threat to be false, so they drag it forward as a test. The Quran answers with a Fire that will not need their invitation.\n\nThe Prophet is not told to produce a small sample of Hell to satisfy them. He is told, by the flow of these verses, that the encompassing is certain. The next line will put words in the scene: reap what you sowed.",
  "55": "\"On the Day the punishment will overwhelm them from above them and from below their feet.\" The encompassing of Hell is now given a geometry. \"Min fawqihim\" from above them, \"wa min tahti arjulihim\" from under their feet. There is no safe sky and no safe floor. Other verses speak of coverings of fire above and below. This line is that covering in two strokes.\n\n\"And it will be said, 'Reap what you sowed.'\" \"Dhuqu ma kuntum ta'malun,\" taste what you used to do. The dare to hasten becomes a meal they must eat. Work returns as flavor. No extra cruelty is announced, only the harvest of their own tilling.\n\nThe voice that says this is not named as a particular angel. It is the verdict of the Day, the same justice that left some people speechless in earlier chapters. Speech now is given to the court, not to the criminals' consultation.\n\nA listener who still has a today should hear that sowing is not finished. The people who repent, believe, and do good were told it is right to hope they will succeed. This verse is the other field, fully ripe.\n\nThe address then turns, with sudden tenderness, to believing servants: My earth is spacious, so worship Me alone. After fire from above and below, the earth is offered as a wide place for tawhid.",
  "56": "\"O My believing servants! My earth is truly spacious, so worship Me alone.\" The tone changes from the Day of covering fire to a call of belonging. \"Ya 'ibadiya alladhina amanu,\" O My servants who have believed. He names them His, and names the earth His: \"ardi,\" My earth.\n\n\"Wide\" is the point. If a town is like Sodom, or like Mecca in persecution, the servant is not told that worship must wait for better politics. The earth has other valleys. Abraham emigrated to his Lord. Moses left for Midian. Early Muslims later left for Abyssinia and Medina. This verse is the charter of that movement.\n\n\"Worship Me alone\" is the reason for using the width. Hijrah is not tourism. It is to keep tawhid when a place makes tawhid impossible or deadly.\n\nA misconception is that every hardship requires leaving home. The verse speaks when worship of Allah alone is the issue, not every discomfort. Another mistake is to think the earth is ownerless land for the taking. It is \"My earth.\" Moving in it is still under His rule.\n\nThe next verse reminds the traveler and the one who stays that every soul will taste death, then to Us you will all be returned. Spacious earth does not mean endless earth. Death makes the journey serious.",
  "57": "\"Every soul will taste death, then to Us you will all be returned.\" After the wide earth comes the one event no emigration outruns. \"Kullu nafsin dha'iqatul-mawt,\" every soul is a taster of death. The wording is sensory. Death is not only an idea. It is tasted, like the punishment that will be tasted by those who sowed it.\n\n\"Then to Us you will all be returned.\" The sequence is fixed: taste, then return. Pharaoh denied the return. This chapter has made the meeting a hope for some and a despair for others. Here it is a fact for every nafs, believing servant and stubborn wrongdoer alike.\n\nThe verse comforts the one told to leave a homeland. Death would have come in Mecca too. It also warns the one who feels safe in a sanctuary. Taste is coming.\n\n\"Soul\" keeps the statement from being only about bodies in graves. The self that said \"we believe,\" the self that dared the punishment, the self that honored parents or obeyed them in shirk, all meet this taste.\n\nThe next verse opens the reward side for those who believe and do good: elevated mansions in Paradise, rivers, forever, an excellent reward for those who work, and then the qualities of patience and trust.",
  "58": "\"As for those who believe and do good, We will certainly house them in elevated mansions in Paradise, under which rivers flow, to stay there forever.\" The servants who may need to leave houses on a spacious earth are promised other houses, \"ghuraf,\" high rooms, in the Garden. Rivers under them repeat the Quran's most familiar picture of permanence and plenty.\n\n\"To stay there forever\" answers the tasting of death. Death is a door, not a demolition of the believer's future home.\n\n\"How excellent is the reward for those who work righteousness!\" The English carries the praise of \"ni'ma ajrul-'amilin,\" how good is the wage of the workers. Faith is not left as a slogan. It is work, \"amal,\" the same pairing this chapter has used since those who believe and do good will have sins absolved.\n\nElevated mansions contrast with the spider's house and with homes in which Midian fell lifeless. Architecture in the Quran is moral. Weak webs, swallowed villas, quake-struck rooms, and high garden chambers are four kinds of shelter.\n\nThe next verse defines the workers with two traits: those who patiently endure, and put their trust in their Lord. Sabr and tawakkul are how a person crosses a spacious earth toward those rooms.",
  "59": "\"Those who patiently endure, and put their trust in their Lord!\" The excellent workers are named by endurance and trust. \"Sabaru\" they practiced sabr: holding the soul to faith when harm comes in Allah's path, when parents pressure, when a town dares punishment, when emigration is required.\n\n\"And put their trust in their Lord\" is tawakkul, placing the weight on the Lord of the spacious earth and of the coming death. The last chapter told the Prophet to put his trust in Allah because he was on clear truth. This verse makes that trust a mark of the people of the high rooms.\n\nThe two qualities fit the chapter's opening. Tests distinguish. Patience is how the truthful stay inside the test. Trust is how they do not take spider-protectors while they wait.\n\nA person can be patient in bitterness without trust, which becomes grim self-reliance. A person can claim trust without patience, which becomes talk. The verse joins them.\n\nThe next question will touch the fear that blocks emigration and trust: how will we eat if we leave? How many creatures cannot secure their own provision, yet Allah feeds them and you.",
  "60": "\"How many are the creatures that cannot secure their provisions!\" \"Dabbah,\" moving creatures, many of them do not carry a storehouse or a plan that could be called securing. Birds, insects, beasts, the weak of the earth, do not lock barns, yet they live.\n\n\"It is Allah Who provides for them and you as well.\" \"Wa iyyakum,\" and you too. The human who worries about leaving Mecca, or about losing a market because he believed, is placed in the same feeding as the creature that cannot secure. Rizq is one office.\n\n\"He is indeed the All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\" He hears the prayer of the distressed in a storm, and the quiet fear of a believer packing to go. He knows where pasture and work will be.\n\nThe verse does not ban work. It bans the theology that provision is owned by a town's idols or by remaining in a place of shirk. Seek, as Abraham said, from Allah. The creatures \"cannot secure,\" yet they are secured.\n\nSolomon's hoopoe and the ant were creatures under provision too. The Quran's world is full of fed lives that never wrote a business plan. The next verses will show that the pagans already admit Allah created the heavens and subjected the sun and moon, then ask how they can be deluded.",
  "61": "\"If you ask them, O Prophet, who created the heavens and the earth and subjected the sun and the moon for your benefit, they will certainly say, 'Allah!'\" Their tongues already know the Creator and the One who made the sun and moon \"musakhkhar,\" pressed into service. The English adds \"for your benefit,\" which is the sense of that subjection for human life, day, night, crops, and travel.\n\n\"How can they then be deluded from the truth?\" \"Yu'fakun,\" how are they turned away? The contradiction is the teaching. They confess Allah as Creator, then give worship, vows, and trust to others. Delusion is not lack of a cosmology lecture. It is a split between admission and devotion.\n\nThe people of Sheba bowed to the sun. These Meccans admit the sun is subjected by Allah, yet still keep partners. Two errors, one family: treating a servant-star as a lord, or admitting the Lord and still associating.\n\nThe question \"if you ask them\" means the proof is inside their own answers. The Prophet does not need to drag a new fact into the valley. He needs them to hear what they already say.\n\nThe next verse states that Allah gives abundant or limited provision to whoever He wills of His servants, and that He has full knowledge of everything. Their confession of the Creator should have included this control of rizq.",
  "62": "\"Allah gives abundant or limited provisions to whoever He wills of His servants.\" The enviers of Korah finally said this when the earth swallowed him. Here it is stated as principle. \"Yabsutu\" He widens, \"yaqdiru\" He measures tightly, for whom He wills among \"ibadih,\" His servants. Both the rich and the pinched remain servants.\n\n\"Surely Allah has full knowledge of everything.\" The widening and tightening are not blind. Knowledge governs the allotment. A tight provision may be a test. A wide one may be a test. The chapter began with tests that sort truthful and liars.\n\nThose who fear they will be snatched from the land if they follow guidance, or who will not emigrate because of food, are being told that the keys of rizq are not in Mecca's idols and not in a particular street. The One they already name as Creator opens and closes.\n\nA misconception is to use this verse to justify laziness or injustice, as if human cheating were Allah's qadr in a way that removes blame. The verse speaks of Allah's giving, not of a license to rob. Shu'ayb still forbade corruption in the land.\n\nThe next question is about rain and the living earth after death. They will again say \"Allah,\" and the Prophet is to say praise be to Allah, while most of them do not understand.",
  "63": "\"And if you ask them who sends down rain from the sky, giving life to the earth after its death, they will surely say, 'Allah!'\" A second interview, same answer. Rain, \"ma',\" from the sky, and revival of dead land, are acts they cannot give to Hubal or to a carved female deity with a straight face. They say Allah.\n\n\"Say, 'Praise be to Allah!'\" The Prophet is to praise, not to sneer. Their correct sentence deserves hamd, because truth has come out of their mouths. Praise also belongs to the One they named.\n\n\"In fact, most of them do not understand.\" \"La ya'qilun.\" They can answer a catechism and still fail to reason from it to tawhid, to trust, to leaving partners. Understanding is connecting the rain to worship, and the revived earth to the revived graves.\n\nThe last chapters used dead earth as a sign of resurrection. This verse puts that sign in their own admission. Most still will not walk the last step.\n\nThe next line ranks the life they cling to: this worldly life is no more than play and amusement, but the Hereafter is the real life, if only they knew. Their answer \"Allah\" should have made them prize the real life He defines.",
  "64": "\"This worldly life is no more than play and amusement.\" \"La'ib\" and \"lahw,\" play and diversion. The sentence does not say the world is unreal as dust under the fingers. It says its role, beside what is coming, is the role of a game: absorbing, then over. Fruits to the sanctuary, parades of Korah, night and day for rest and bounty, all can become lahw if they hide the return.\n\n\"But the Hereafter is indeed the real life, if only they knew.\" \"Al-hayawan,\" the life that is truly life. The English \"real life\" captures that intensification. If they knew, they would not dare the punishment, or refuse emigration, or treat rain's God as one name among others.\n\n\"If only they knew\" matches Abraham's \"if only you knew\" and the spider verse's \"if only they knew.\" Knowledge in this surah is the missing link, not information about crops.\n\nA misconception is to read \"play\" as a ban on marriage, work, and joy. Other verses command taking a share of the world and being good as Allah was good. Play is the world when it is taken as the serious home. The high rooms of Paradise are the serious home.\n\nThe next image is a ship in a storm: they cry to Allah alone with sincere devotion, then associate again on shore. The real life is forgotten as soon as the waves calm.",
  "65": "\"If they happen to be aboard a ship caught in a storm, they cry out to Allah alone in sincere devotion.\" The sea strips the shop of idols. \"Mukhlisina lahu al-din,\" making the religion sincere for Him. In that hour they become, in practice, people of tawhid. The mudtarr, the distressed, of an earlier chapter appears on a deck.\n\n\"But as soon as He delivers them safely to shore, they associate others with Him once again.\" Delivery, the same word used for Noah and Lot and Moses' mother, is met with shirk. Safety returns the partners. Sincerity was a weather sincerity.\n\nThe verse is a mirror, not only a taunt at sailors. Many people pray like monotheists in a hospital and bargain like polytheists in a market. The ship is any place where ropes fail.\n\nAllah still delivers. His mercy is not reserved for those who will stay grateful. That makes the later unbelief heavier, not lighter.\n\nThe next verse lets them be ungrateful and enjoy for now, for they will soon know. It is a release that is also a threat, the rope of time before the surprise punishment.",
  "66": "\"So let them be ungrateful for all We have given them, and let them enjoy themselves for now!\" The tone is the terrible permission of one who is not anxious. \"Fal-yakfuru,\" so let them deny the favor. \"Wal-yatamatta'u,\" and let them take their mata', their short enjoyment. \"For now\" is the English of a enjoyment that has an expiry.\n\n\"For they will soon know.\" \"Fa-sawfa ya'lamun.\" Knowledge will arrive. It is the knowledge of the Day, or of a sudden punishment, the knowledge that comes when the shore-partners fail and Hell encompasses.\n\nThis is not a command for believers to encourage sin. It is Allah's way of speaking when advice has been refused: take the rope. The chapter has already said evildoers think they will escape, and how bad is their judgment.\n\nThe gifts they will be ungrateful for include the ship that reached shore, the rain they attributed correctly, the sanctuary of the next verse, and the recited Book they said was not enough.\n\nThe next question asks whether they have not seen that Mecca was made a safe haven while people around them are snatched away, and how they can then believe in falsehood and deny Allah's favours.",
  "67": "\"Have they not seen how We have made Mecca a safe haven, whereas people all around them are snatched away?\" The last chapter answered the fear \"we would be snatched from our land\" by pointing at the sanctuary. This verse points again. \"Haram aminan,\" a safe inviolable place. Around them, in the raids and wars of Arabia, people are \"yutakhattaf,\" snatched. Their own eyes know the contrast.\n\n\"How can they then believe in falsehood and deny Allah's favours?\" Two acts: iman in batil, and kufr of \"ni'mah,\" the favors. The safety of Mecca is a ni'mah from the Lord of the city, not from the partners housed in the Kaaba. To credit the partners is to believe in falsehood. To refuse thanks to Allah is to deny the favors.\n\nThe argument is local and tight. They will not follow guidance for fear of being snatched, while Allah has already made them the un-snatched people of the peninsula. The fear contradicts the favor they inhabit.\n\nSeeing is again commanded. A sanctuary is as visible as a ruin. Both are signs. The next verse asks who does more wrong than those who fabricate lies against Allah or reject the truth after it has reached them, and whether Hell is not a fitting home for the disbelievers.",
  "68": "\"And who does more wrong than those who fabricate lies against Allah or reject the truth after it has reached them?\" Two peaks of zulm: \"iftara,\" inventing against Allah—partners, the claim that angels are His daughters, the lie that He has a child—and \"kadhdhaba bil-haqq,\" calling the truth a lie after it \"ja'ahu,\" has come to him. The recited Book is that arrived truth.\n\nThe question \"who does more wrong\" is a scale with an empty higher pan. These two crimes fill the top.\n\n\"Is Hell not a fitting home for the disbelievers?\" \"Mathwa,\" a dwelling. The high rooms of Paradise were a home for workers of good. Hell is asked about as a home that fits those who invent and reject. The question expects yes.\n\n\"After it has reached them\" removes the excuse of never hearing. This chapter spent its middle on warners to nation after nation. Mecca has a warner and a Book said to be enough.\n\nThe last verse of the chapter will turn from this dark home to the people who struggle in Our cause: We will guide them along Our Way, and Allah is with the good-doers. Wrongdoing has a house. Struggle has company.",
  "69": "\"As for those who struggle in Our cause, We will surely guide them along Our Way.\" The chapter that began with tests ends with \"jahadu fina,\" those who strive in Us, in Our path. Struggle here is the effort of the truthful in fitnah: holding faith, emigrating if needed, refusing parents in shirk, reciting, praying, arguing gracefully. It is not limited to a battlefield, though it can include that when later commanded.\n\n\"We will surely guide them along Our Way.\" Guidance is the fruit, not always the starting comfort. \"Subulana,\" Our ways or Our Way, is the road Allah opens for the one already moving. Other verses say He increases in guidance those who are guided. This line is that increase as a promise.\n\n\"And Allah is certainly with the good-doers.\" \"Ma'a al-muhsinin,\" with those who do ihsan, excellence in worship and in dealing. With-ness is better than a spider-protector. Moses was told he was secure. Abraham was saved from fire. The good-doers are not promised a life without tests. They are promised company.\n\nThe verse answers the one who fears that struggle will leave him lost. Movement in Allah's cause is itself a reason for more light. The next chapter will open with letters and a report about Romans defeated and soon to triumph, a worldly event that believers will rejoice at as a victory willed by Allah. Struggle and history will meet."
};
